>

  <h2>the brief</h2>

  <p>
    Build a digital plan for an online perfume retailer, and then actually build the thing, so the strategy has to
    survive contact with a real interface rather than staying on slides.
  </p>

  <h2>approach</h2>

  <h3>One measurable goal</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Increase online sales by 15% by the end of the year</strong> in the Serbian market, with eight supporting
    strategies mapped underneath it: site design, advertising spend, range, product detail, promotional campaign,
    points collection, loyalty programme and sample gifts. One number at the top, so everything below it could be
    judged on whether it moved that number.
  </p>

  <h3>Two audiences, sized</h3>
  <p>
    A primary persona, Marija, 27, a digital specialist who invests in quality and needs to save time, and a
    secondary one, Nikola, 35, an interior designer who buys on aesthetics and wants to stay current. Target
    population sized at <strong>288,830 employed women</strong> from national statistics office ICT usage data.
  </p>

  <h3>Competitive position</h3>
  <p>
    Three competitors assessed on what they were missing: two selling cosmetics rather than specialising, one with
    no editorial content at all, one online-only. The gap we targeted was simplicity, pleasant browsing and a site
    that is easy to search, rather than competing on range or price.
  </p>

  <h3>Work the framework against a real build</h3>
  <p>
    We took the 7C framework and used each element as a requirement for the site we were building, rather than as a
    description written afterwards:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Context</strong>, visual theme, simple navigation, mobile adaptation</li>
    <li><strong>Content</strong>, a scent-matching questionnaire and a blog that answers real questions</li>
    <li><strong>Community</strong>, blog comments so customers talk to each other, not only to us</li>
    <li><strong>Customization</strong>, personalised recommendations, offers and a blog calendar</li>
    <li><strong>Communication</strong>, both audiences addressed at once, with contact routes that actually work</li>
    <li><strong>Connection</strong>, links out to social channels and brand pages</li>
    <li><strong>Commerce</strong>, cart, coupon handling, order and delivery tracking</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Map the journey, then schedule the work</h3>
  <p>
    A nine-step customer journey from intent through to leaving a review, which exposed where a user could fall out
    of the process. Then a multi-week editorial calendar with format, day, copy, hashtags and a named owner per
    post.
  </p>

  <div class="rc-result">
    <p class="rc-result-label">why the calendar matters</p>
    <p>
      Assigning an owner and a date to every planned post is the difference between a content strategy and a content
      plan. It is the least glamorous artefact in the project and the only one that determines whether any of it
      happens.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>what came out of it</h2>

  <p>
    A working WordPress store with a scent-finder questionnaire, blog, cart, coupon codes and checkout, built to
    match the plan, plus the content schedule to run it. The site is informational as well as transactional, which
    was the deliberate choice: the blog and the questionnaire are what bring someone in before they are ready to buy.
  </p>

  <p class="rc-note">
    Team project with four colleagues. I was involved across all of it, and this is also where the marketing side
    and the development side of what I do met for the first time.
  </p>

</RecruiterLayout>
